Until a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/03/apple-accidentally-leaks-macbook-neo/">last-minute leak</a> revealed the MacBook Neo name, it was widely assumed that Apple's lower-cost MacBook would simply be named "MacBook." After all, Apple offered a plain "MacBook" from 2006 to 2012, and again from 2015 to 2019. In the end, Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/04/apple-announces-low-cost-macbook-neo-with-a18-pro-chip/">did go with MacBook Neo branding</a>, and it has explained why in a new interview.<br/>
<br/>
